title: '[pallet-revive] Serve the ETH RPC from inside the node'

doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    A `polkadot-omni-node` built with the new `experimental-eth-rpc-in-node` compile-time
    feature can serve the Ethereum JSON-RPC endpoint itself, instead of running a separate
    `eth-rpc` process over a loopback WebSocket. Pass `--eth-rpc` to start it; without the
    flag the node behaves exactly as one built without the feature. It listens on
    `--eth-rpc-port` (8545 by default), on localhost unless `--eth-rpc-external` is given.
    Experimental: the in-process transport between the embedded server and the node (how
    it dispatches JSON-RPC calls into the node without a socket) is still being reworked
    and may change.

- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` gains `in_process::InProcessRpcClient`, a `subxt` `RpcClientT`
    that dispatches into a node's in-memory `RpcModule` instead of a socket, and
    `in_process::start_embedded`, which builds the ETH RPC client on it and spawns the
    server on an existing `TaskManager`. Both are gated behind the crate's own
    `experimental-eth-rpc-in-node` feature, off by default, which `polkadot-omni-node-lib`
    forwards under the same feature name.

    The subxt metadata the crate generates its client from is now checked in as
    `revive_chain.scale` rather than dumped by the build script, so `pallet-revive-eth-rpc`
    no longer build-depends on `revive-dev-runtime`. A unit test keeps the file in step with
    the runtime's `metadata_at_version(16)`; run it with `BLESS_METADATA=1` to refresh it.

crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: major
- name: polkadot-omni-node-lib
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-omni-node
  bump: patch
